---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import VideoTabs from '../../components/VideoTabs.astro';
---
<Base title="TrafficWhatIf" description="A data-centric benchmark for intervention-aware traffic prediction: predicting traffic after the road network changes.">
  <ProjectHeader
    title="TrafficWhatIf"
    subtitle="What happens to city traffic after we change the roads? Building the data to learn that."
    status="Early stage · ongoing"
    role="Team lead / researcher, USC InfoLab"
    period="Sep 2026 – present"
    team="Learned-simulator direction developed with Prof. Cyrus Shahabi and collaborators; I lead the data and benchmark design."
    tags={['Synthetic data', 'Sim-to-real transfer', 'Graph-structured shift', 'Surrogate models']}
    tldr={[
      'Standard traffic forecasting predicts the future from the past on a <strong>fixed</strong> road network. Planners need the counterfactual: what traffic looks like <strong>after</strong> a closure or policy change.',
      'Real data for that question barely exist, so the project starts by <strong>designing the data</strong>, working backwards from the learning problem to the observations it needs.',
      'Targets: generalization to <strong>unseen cities</strong> and <strong>transfer from simulation to the real world</strong>. This is work in progress; no results are claimed yet.',
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The gap</h2>
  <p>
    A traffic department’s main lever is the road network itself: closing a lane, changing a turn restriction, rerouting around an event.
    Microscopic simulators can answer “what if?”, but they are slow at city scale. A learned model could answer in seconds and let planners
    explore many options. Training one needs <em>intervention-aware</em> data, meaning traffic observed before and after known changes to the
    network. Real-world data of this kind are rare, partial, and usually inferred after the fact, for example by guessing that a road was closed
    because its flow dropped to zero.
  </p>

  <h2>Reversing the usual workflow</h2>
  <div class="flip">
    <div><span class="k">Usual</span><p>dataset → choose a model → hope it generalizes</p></div>
    <div class="hi"><span class="k">Here</span><p>learning problem → observations it requires → a designed data-generating process</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>
    I am currently designing a data-centric benchmark for intervention-aware traffic prediction. Models see recent traffic observations
    together with a changed road graph and predict post-intervention traffic. The design aims for data that are realistic where it matters
    for the task, controllable, and reproducible. The generator itself is meant to be released and modified, so other groups can vary its
    assumptions and study how those choices affect what downstream models learn.
  </p>

  <VideoTabs
    items={[
      { label: 'Pasadena', src: '/media/trafficwhatif/pasadena.mp4', poster: '/media/trafficwhatif/pasadena.webp', alt: 'Simulated road-sensor occupancy and speed over Pasadena across a day' },
      { label: 'Singapore', src: '/media/trafficwhatif/singapore.mp4', poster: '/media/trafficwhatif/singapore.webp', alt: 'Simulated road-sensor occupancy and speed over Singapore across a day' },
      { label: 'Rotterdam', src: '/media/trafficwhatif/rotterdam.mp4', poster: '/media/trafficwhatif/rotterdam.webp', alt: 'Simulated road-sensor occupancy and speed over Rotterdam across a day' },
    ]}
    caption="Early generated data: simulated road-sensor occupancy and speed over a day in three cities (5-minute frames). Grey = no traffic; green → red = rising occupancy; thicker = slower."
  />

  <h2>How I will know whether the data are any good</h2>
  <p>
    “Realistic” is hard to test directly, so the test is about what the data teach. Train <em>several different model families</em> on
    the synthetic data and evaluate all of them on matched real-world intervention cases.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>If diverse models transfer well, the synthetic data contain task-relevant structure that carries over to the real world.</li>
    <li>If diverse models all learn the synthetic task but fail in the real world, the problem is the data-generation assumptions, not one weak model, and those assumptions need revising.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where it came from</h2>
  <p>
    A pilot project, <strong>TrafficDataGen</strong>, simulated traffic on a 10 × 10 grid city with thousands of agents, each given a randomized daily
    schedule. It showed that conventional simulators are slow at this scale, and its way of generating agent schedules became the starting
    point for how TrafficWhatIf generates agent plans.
  </p>

  <p class="muted small">
    This is unpublished work in progress, so the page describes the research direction only.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .flip { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 12px; margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem; }
  .flip > div { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-sm); padding: 12px 16px; background: var(--surface); }
  .flip .hi { border-color: var(--accent); background: var(--accent-soft); }
  .flip .k { font-size: .7rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .flip .hi .k { color: var(--accent-ink); }
  .flip p { margin: .25rem 0 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.05rem; }
  @media (max-width: 639px) { .flip { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
